{
  "id": "parental-investment-theory",
  "term": "parental investment theory",
  "definition": "Parental investment theory is a social theory used to explain gender differences in behavior by proposing different sensitivities to risk and betrayal. In the context of trust, it predicts that women will tend to trust less because social risk matters more to them, whereas men will tend to trust more to pursue resources and signal willingness to accept potential loss.",
